#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_dsolve

#% text
# `n_dsolve(eqs, y, [t, t0, t1])` solves the ordinary differential equations `eqs` with their initial conditions numerically for `t` from `t0` to `t1`, and gives `[y -> f]` with `f` an interpolating function.

#% text
# `n_dsolve(eqs, [y, z, ...], [t, t0, t1])` solves a system for several unknown functions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n_dsolve(eqs, u, [t, t0, t1], [x, a, b])` solves a partial differential equation in t and x by the method of lines: one equation for `d(u(t, x), t)` (or the second derivative in t) in terms of u, its first and second derivatives in x, t and x; the initial values `u(t0, x) == f(x)` (and of `derivative(1, 0)(u)(t0, x)` for a second derivative in t); one condition at each end, `u(t, a) == g(t)` or `derivative(0, 1)(u)(t, a) == g(t)`. The interval [a, b] is divided into 100 equal parts, the derivatives in x are differences of fourth order in the spacing (five-point central ones, one-sided ones next to an end with a condition `u(t, a) == g(t)`; three-point ones at an end with a condition on the derivative), and the values at the nodes are solved as a system of ordinary equations; the result `interpolating_function("u", [[t0, t1], [a, b]], ...)` takes `(t, x)` and interpolates in x by cubics through the nearest nodes. Other forms stay unevaluated.

#% item
# An unknown whose initial value is a list, `z(0) == [1, 0]`, is vector-valued: equations between lists hold component by component, and its interpolating function gives the list of the components' values.

#% item
# Derivatives are written with primes: `y'(t)`, `y''(t)`. Initial conditions give the value of each unknown, and of its derivatives up to one less than the order, at `t0`.

#% item
# The result is a rule list; `y(t) /. sol` evaluates the solution at a point, and a plot of it draws the curve.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `method: auto | rk45 | stiff | taylor`: the integration method (`auto` changes to the stiff method when the problem needs it).

#% subitem
# `accuracy_goal`, `precision_goal`, `max_steps`, `max_step_size`: the error goals and the limits of the steps.

#% subitem
# `events: [g == 0]` stops the solution where `g` becomes zero (with `event_action: record`, it records the points and goes on).

#% subitem
# `digits: d` solves with Taylor series at `d` decimal digits; values of the solution are then balls.

#% subitem
# `evaluation_monitor: f` and `step_monitor: f` call `f` with the value of the independent variable at every evaluation of the equations and at the end of every accepted step; under `digits:` the Taylor method expands the equations once per step, at the working precision, and integrates twice (the second run estimates the error), so the monitors see both runs. A failure `f` gives ends the integration and is the result.

#% item
# When the step size shrinks to nothing (the solution blows up), the solution covers the part computed and a message says where it stopped.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Exponential decay, evaluated at t = 1:

#% cell
s = n_dsolve([y'(t) == -y(t), y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 2])

#% cell
y(1) /. s
#% output
#= 0.3678794409408397

#% text
# The solution is an interpolating function over the interval:

#% cell
s
#% output
#= [y -> interpolating_function("y", [0.0, 2.0], 21, "7d83dd8f58ce332a")]

#% text
# The logistic equation, and a plot of its solution:

#% cell
logistic = n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t)*(1 - y(t)), y(0) == 0.1], y, [t, 0, 10])

#% cell
plot(y(t) /. logistic, [t, 0, 10])
#% output
#= image("n_dsolve.files/n_dsolve-5.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A second-order equation, compared with its exact solution sin(t):

#% cell
s2 = n_dsolve([y''(t) + y(t) == 0, y(0) == 0, y'(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 6])

#% cell
(y(2) /. s2) - sin(2)
#% output
#= -5.187932750061464e-9

#% text
# A system of two first-order equations, an oscillator, after half a period:

#% cell
osc = n_dsolve([x'(t) == v(t), v'(t) == -x(t), x(0) == 1, v(0) == 0], [x, v], [t, 0, 10])

#% cell
x(pi) /. osc
#% output
#= -0.9999999909648007

#% text
# The solution at several points at once:

#% cell
[y(0), y(5), y(10)] /. logistic
#% output
#= [0.1, 0.9428256207705609, 0.9995915655941424]

#% text
# A vector-valued unknown: a rotation in the plane.

#% cell
rot = n_dsolve([z'(t) == dot([[0, 1], [-1, 0]], z(t)), z(0) == [1, 0]], z, [t, 0, 10])

#% cell
z(1) /. rot
#% output
#= [0.5403023042030178, -0.8414709830825576]

#% text
# The heat equation on [0, 1] with an oscillating temperature at one end:

#% cell
heat = n_dsolve([d(u(t, x), t) == d(u(t, x), x, x), u(0, x) == 0, u(t, 0) == sin(t), u(t, 1) == 0], u, [t, 0, 5], [x, 0, 1])

#% cell
u(5, 0.5) /. heat
#% output
#= -0.4908258637125183

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The times of the accepted steps, the first few:

#% cell
take(last(reap(n_dsolve([x''(t) + sin(x(t)) == 0, x(0) == 1, x'(0) == 0], x, [t, 0, 10], step_monitor: (t => sow(t)))))[1], 4)
#% output
#= [0.010351235061936524, 0.07132228440263969, 0.15149778355017396, 0.25376759367657736]

#% text
# A stiff equation, with the stiff method:

#% cell
stiff = n_dsolve([y'(t) == -1000*(y(t) - cos(t)), y(0) == 0], y, [t, 0, 1], method: stiff)

#% cell
y(1) /. stiff
#% output
#= 0.5411432357946486

#% text
# High precision with Taylor series:

#% cell
s3 = n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t), y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 1], digits: 30)

#% cell
y(1) /. s3
#% output
#= 2.718281828459045235360287471353

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A ball dropped from 10 m: the event stops the solution when it reaches the ground, and records the time.

#% cell
fall = n_dsolve([h'(t) == v(t), v'(t) == -9.81, h(0) == 10, v(0) == 0], [h, v], [t, 0, 5], events: [h(t) == 0])

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The solution of y' = y^2 with y(0) = 1 is 1/(1 - t), which blows up at t = 1; the solution stops there:

#% cell
n_dsolve([y'(t) == y(t)^2, y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 2])
#% output
#| n_dsolve: the step size vanished (a singularity?) at t = 1.0000000010762093; the solution covers the part computed
#= [y -> interpolating_function("y", [0.0, 1.0000000010762093], 501, "7e5099b7fca175ab")]

#% text
# Without initial conditions there is no single solution to compute:

#% cell
n_dsolve([y'(t) == -y(t)], y, [t, 0, 1])
#% output
#! failure(argument, n_dsolve, "no initial conditions")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_integrate`, `plot`, `find_root`, `d`
